// PosixRuntime — the POSIX host surface as DATA: the symbol names its chunks wear, the constants their
// bodies read, and the one description of `struct stat` those bodies are parameterised by. Nothing here
// emits an instruction, which is exactly why it is arch-neutral: every declaration is a fact about POSIX or
// about the Win32 surface being emulated, and neither has an opinion about AArch64 or x86-64.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THREE BACKENDS' LANES READ IT** — arm64-macOS, arm64-Linux and x64-Linux. A fact declared beside one
// of those lanes and read by another is this project's signature bug, ONE FACT WRITTEN DOWN TWICE, in the
// one place where a drift shows up as a wrong SIZE or a wrong TIMESTAMP rather than as a compile error.
// Per-arch facts stay with their arch (`Targets/{Arm64,X64}/…`), Linux-but-arch-neutral ones are
// `Targets/Shared/LinuxRuntime.maxon`, and the emit fragments that consume all three stay with their
// assembler.
//
// ⭐ **THE SYMBOLS ARE `mrt_host_*`, NOT `mrt_darwin_*`/`mrt_linux_*`, AND THAT IS WHY NO ISEL HAS A NAME
// TABLE.** Exactly ONE lane's chunks are appended to any image (each backend's builder picks by
// `target.os`), so a shared name cannot collide — it can only ever have one body — and it is what lets
// `lowerHostChunkValue(chunk: HostReadFileName)` stay a single unconditional lowering. The alternative,
// per-lane names, needs a second table mapping `(os, verb)` to a symbol, which is a table that can disagree
// with the builders. `mrt_start` and `mrt_panic` already work this way: one name, a per-OS body.
//
// ⚠ **A LANE-PRIVATE HELPER KEEPS ITS LANE'S INFIX.** `mrt_darwin_dup_cstring` is a `malloc` and
// `mrt_linux_glob_match` exists only because a raw static image has no `fnmatch`; neither has a counterpart
// on every lane, so neither may wear the shared prefix. The test is whether the VERB belongs to the shared
// host surface, not whether every lane happens to spell it as a chunk.

// ==========================================================================
// The shared chunk symbols
// ==========================================================================

// The ones an isel branches to, plus the four the find-first walks are built out of. Each is built by
// whichever lane's `append…RuntimeChunks` ran.
//
// ⚠ **`mrt_host_file_open` AND `mrt_host_cpu_count` ARE BUILT ONLY BY THE LINUX LANES**, and each isel is
// what keeps that honest: it `bl`s them on the SYSCALL route alone, because the macOS route has no chunk to
// call — an `open` there is one inline import (`lowerOsFileOpen` carries the vararg argument for why) and a
// processor count is one `sysconf`.
export let HostLastErrorName = b"mrt_host_last_error"
export let HostReadFileName = b"mrt_host_read_file"
export let HostWriteFileName = b"mrt_host_write_file"
export let HostFileOpenName = b"mrt_host_file_open"
export let HostFileSizeName = b"mrt_host_file_size"
export let HostFileDeleteName = b"mrt_host_file_delete"
export let HostFileRenameName = b"mrt_host_file_rename"
export let HostDirCreateName = b"mrt_host_dir_create"
export let HostFileAttributesName = b"mrt_host_file_attributes"
export let HostDirCurrentPathName = b"mrt_host_dir_current_path"
export let HostDirFindFirstName = b"mrt_host_dir_find_first"
export let HostDirFindNextName = b"mrt_host_dir_find_next"
export let HostDirFindCloseName = b"mrt_host_dir_find_close"

// The shared walkers the two find-first bodies are built out of. They are runtime FUNCTIONS rather than emit
// fragments because each has more than one caller and each is a LOOP: inlining a loop at three sites is
// three copies of a termination condition.
export let HostBaseNameName = b"mrt_host_base_name"
export let HostHasWildcardName = b"mrt_host_has_wildcard"
export let HostAttrsFromModeName = b"mrt_host_attrs_from_mode"
export let HostFillFindDataName = b"mrt_host_fill_find_data"

// --- The two non-scheduler CLOCKS, the executable path and the processor count ---
//
// Read the calendar, read this thread's CPU, name the running image, count the processors — host-surface
// verbs in exactly the sense this file's header defines, so they wear the shared prefix like every name
// above.
export let HostWallClockName = b"mrt_host_wall_clock"
export let HostThreadCpuName = b"mrt_host_thread_cpu"
export let HostExePathName = b"mrt_host_exe_path"
export let HostCpuCountName = b"mrt_host_cpu_count"

// --- Is this descriptor a TERMINAL? ---
//
// ⭐ **A CHUNK ON EVERY POSIX LANE, WHERE THE Std OP IS ONE IMPORT CALL ON WINDOWS.** `GetFileType` takes a
// handle and answers a word; the POSIX question needs either a call whose upper half must be narrowed
// (`isatty`) or an `ioctl` with a `struct termios` the CALLER must own — and a frame for that buffer is
// exactly what a chunk has and an isel lowering does not.
export let HostHandleFileTypeName = b"mrt_host_handle_file_type"

// --- The GREEN-THREAD FLOOR's host objects, and the two clocks the scheduler reaches ---
//
// ⭐⭐ **TEN VERBS, TWO BODIES EACH.** macOS answers every one of them with a libSystem sequence (a pthread
// key, a mutex, a condvar, a thread, `getenv`, `nanosleep`, `clock_gettime_nsec_np`); arm64-Linux answers
// each with a raw `svc #0` against `futex`, `mmap` and `clock_gettime`, plus the writable thread pointer
// nothing else on that lane touches. The verbs are identical and the primitives share nothing, which is
// exactly the split the `mrt_host_` prefix exists for.
//
// ⚠ **`osLockEnter`/`osLockLeave` ARE NOT HERE, AND THAT IS THE LANE-PRIVATE RULE APPLIED HONESTLY.** On
// macOS the region the Std tier allocated IS a `pthread_mutex_t`, so those two ops lower to a bare import
// call with no chunk at all; only Linux needs a body, and a body only one lane builds is
// `mrt_linux_lock_enter` (see `Arm64LinuxRuntime`). The same is true of `tlsSetValue`, which is one
// `pthread_setspecific` there and a guarded store through `TPIDR_EL0` on arm64-Linux.
export let HostMonotonicClockName = b"mrt_host_monotonic_clock"
export let HostSleepMsName = b"mrt_host_sleep_ms"
export let HostWaitNewName = b"mrt_host_wait_new"
export let HostEventSetName = b"mrt_host_event_set"
export let HostThreadCreateName = b"mrt_host_thread_create"
export let HostWaitOneName = b"mrt_host_wait_one"
export let HostWaitCloseName = b"mrt_host_wait_close"
export let HostLockInitName = b"mrt_host_lock_init"
export let HostEnvReadName = b"mrt_host_env_read"

export let HostTlsAllocName = b"mrt_host_tls_alloc"

// ⭐⭐ **WHO REACHES THE HOST-OBJECT BAND — the reap, the close and the lock init — AND IT IS WIDER THAN THE
// SCHEDULER'S BIT.** `__ds_init` guards the ring with `osLockInit`/`osLockEnter`/`osLockLeave` and closes the
// section descriptor the moment the mapping exists, and `__shm_close` closes one with no lock and no
// scheduler anywhere, so both ends of a named segment reach the band without a green thread. The reap rides
// it rather than a gate of its own: `mrt_host_wait_close` dispatches to it for the process KIND of handle,
// and a call to an absent symbol is a link failure rather than a diagnostic.
//
// ⚠ **ALL THREE POSIX LANES ASK IT AND NONE OWNS IT** — the question is about `RuntimeUsage` and names no
// instruction set, exactly as `LinuxRuntime`'s four gates record for their bands. A copy beside one
// assembler is a producer added to one lane's gate and not the others'.
export function posixUsesHostObjects(usage RuntimeUsage) returns bool
	return usage.usesGt or usage.usesDebugStream or usage.usesSharedMemory
end 'posixUsesHostObjects'

// ⭐⭐ **WHO EMITS `StdOp.osEnvRead` — the union both env-read gates on a POSIX lane read.** Two producers
// and they are independent: `SchedRuntime.emitResolveMaxProcs` reads `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` in every
// green-thread program, and `TerminalRuntime.buildTerminalStdoutWantsColor` reads `NO_COLOR` and `TERM` in
// every program that asks whether stdout wants colour, which need not have a scheduler. Gated on `usesGt`
// alone the chunk is a call to a symbol nothing laid out — MEASURED as `arm64ResolveCallFixups: bl to
// unknown function 'mrt_host_env_read'` on the first terminal-detecting program built for a POSIX lane.
export function posixReadsEnvironment(usage RuntimeUsage) returns bool
	return usage.usesGt or usage.usesTerminalDetection
end 'posixReadsEnvironment'

// --- The CHILD-PROCESS band ---
//
// `mrt_host_proc_reap` and `mrt_host_proc_await` are internal to the band — no isel lowering names either —
// but they are `bl`'d from bodies more than one lane lays out, so they wear the shared prefix for the same
// reason every name above does: a per-lane infix would need a table mapping `(os, verb)` to a symbol.
export let HostPipeName = b"mrt_host_pipe"
export let HostPipePeekName = b"mrt_host_pipe_peek"
export let HostSpawnName = b"mrt_host_spawn"
export let HostProcReapName = b"mrt_host_proc_reap"
export let HostProcAwaitName = b"mrt_host_proc_await"
export let HostProcExitCodeName = b"mrt_host_proc_exit_code"
export let HostProcTerminateName = b"mrt_host_proc_terminate"
export let HostWaitAnyName = b"mrt_host_wait_any"
export let HostTickMsName = b"mrt_host_tick_ms"

// ⚠ **THE MILLISECOND STOPWATCH HAS THREE PRODUCERS AND ONLY ONE IS IN THE PIPE BAND.** A subprocess
// deadline reads it, every DebugStream entry stamps `now - start_timestamp`, and `__uptime_ms` is the
// language's own door onto it — so the gate is their union, which is what lets `TargetFacilities` claim
// `HostFacility.uptimeClock` on a POSIX lane.
export function posixReadsUptimeClock(usage RuntimeUsage) returns bool
	return usage.usesIocp or usage.usesDebugStream or usage.usesUptimeClock
end 'posixReadsUptimeClock'

// ⚠ **ONE CLOCK, TWO CALLERS, SO ONE GATE THAT IS THEIR UNION.** `mrt_host_tick_ms` derives its
// milliseconds from `mrt_host_monotonic_clock` rather than reading a second clock, and the scheduler's
// deadlines come from that chunk directly — so a program with either reaches it, and gating it on the
// scheduler alone leaves a traced program branching to a symbol nothing laid out. DERIVED from its own
// subset so the two cannot come apart.
export function posixReadsMonotonicClock(usage RuntimeUsage) returns bool
	return usage.usesGt or posixReadsUptimeClock(usage)
end 'posixReadsMonotonicClock'

// ⭐ **THE TWO MEMBERS OF THE NAMED-SECTION BAND THAT ARE ONE BODY ON EVERY POSIX LANE.** A segment is
// mapped and unmapped by `mmap`/`munmap` over a descriptor, which differ only in how the call reaches the
// kernel — so they take a `PosixHostCall` and wear the shared prefix. ⚠ The CREATE and the ATTACH do not:
// `shm_open` is a libSystem call on Darwin and a libc FUNCTION on Linux, where the substrate opens the
// `/dev/shm` path that function would have built — two different bodies, so two lane-private names.
export let HostShmMapName = b"mrt_host_shm_map"
export let HostShmUnmapName = b"mrt_host_shm_unmap"

// ⭐⭐ **THE BAND IS GATED ON THE UNION OF ITS TWO ENDS.** `usesSharedMemory` is the CONSUMER's bit
// (`stdlib/SharedMemory.maxon` reaching `__shm_*`), `usesDebugStream` the PRODUCER's — and the band is laid
// out WHOLE because its members reach each other: `__ds_init` opens and maps where `__shm_close` unmaps and
// unpublishes, and on a Linux lane all three named bodies call the path builder. Splitting the gate leaves
// one end branching to a symbol nothing emitted.
export function posixUsesNamedSection(usage RuntimeUsage) returns bool
	return usage.usesSharedMemory or usage.usesDebugStream
end 'posixUsesNamedSection'

// ==========================================================================
// The POSIX facts every lane reads
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐ **THESE ARE POSIX-COMMON AND WERE MEASURED AS SUCH, NOT ASSUMED.** `S_IFMT`/`S_IFDIR`/`S_IWUSR` are the
// same octal on Darwin and on Linux — unlike the `clockid_t` values, which are NOT and which is why that
// block stays with its lane.
export let PosixFileTypeMask = 0o170000
export let PosixFileTypeDirectory = 0o40000
export let PosixOwnerWriteBit = 0o200

// `st_mode` is a `mode_t`. Darwin's is SIXTEEN bits inside the 32-bit word at its offset (the other half is
// `st_nlink`); Linux's is a full 32-bit `unsigned int`. One mask serves both because every bit any lane
// reads — the type field and the owner's write bit — is inside the low 16, so masking a Linux mode to 16
// bits discards nothing this surface asks about.
export let PosixModeMask = 0xFFFF

// The two bytes that make a pattern a GLOB rather than a name, and the byte a path is split at. `*` and `?`
// are the only two wildcard characters `FindFirstFileA` honours; a pattern with neither is a plain path and
// takes the single-`stat` route.
export let PosixGlobStarByte = 0x2A
export let PosixGlobQuestionByte = 0x3F
export let PosixPathSeparatorByte = 0x2F

// `fstatat`'s flag word: 0 means FOLLOW a symbolic link, which is what `FindFirstFileA` reports for one and
// what every other `stat` in this surface does.
export let PosixFollowSymlinks = 0

// `O_RDONLY` — zero on every POSIX lane, and named because a bare 0 in an open's flag position says nothing.
export let PosixOpenReadOnly = 0

// `O_RDWR` — the access mode a SHARED SEGMENT is opened with at both ends, because the ring's consumer
// writes its header and cursors and its producer writes entries. POSIX-universal, unlike the CREATION
// flags beside it at the create site, which each lane numbers for itself.
export let PosixOpenReadWrite = 2

// The permission masks a newly created file is given, before the process umask. 0644 is `CreateFileA`'s
// ordinary file; 0755 is what `openWriteExecutable` adds and the whole reason that method has an op of its own.
export let PosixCreateFileMode = 0o644
export let PosixCreateExecutableFileMode = 0o755

// `mkdir(path, 0777)` — `CreateDirectoryA`'s "inherit whatever the environment permits", which under POSIX is
// a full mask handed to the process umask to narrow. The bootstrap's own directory create says 0777 and v1's
// says 0755; 0777 is the one that leaves the DECISION with the umask instead of taking it here.
export let PosixDirectoryMode = 0o777

// ⭐⭐ **THE errno VALUES `mrt_host_last_error` TRANSLATES — AND ALL FIVE AGREE ACROSS EVERY LANE.**
// MEASURED on Darwin (`<errno.h>`) and read off Linux's `asm-generic/errno-base.h`: `EPERM = 1`,
// `ENOENT = 2`, `EACCES = 13`, `ENOTDIR = 20`, `ERANGE = 34` on both. Neither AArch64 nor x86-64 overrides
// that header — each arch's `asm/errno.h` is a bare include of it — so the Linux half is one table, not two.
// That agreement is what lets the TRANSLATION be shared while the FETCH stays per-lane (`*__error()` on
// Darwin, a `.data` word or a scheduler slot on Linux).
//
// ⛔⛔ **ONLY `ENOENT` AGREES WITH ITS Win32 TWIN BY LUCK, AND THE OTHER TWO WOULD MISCLASSIFY.** The Std
// tier tests 2 / 3 / 5 (`ManagedFileRuntime.emitErrnoClassifiedThrow`); 3 is `ESRCH` and 5 is `EIO` under
// POSIX, so handing it a raw errno would report a hardware read error as `accessDenied` and would never
// report a missing directory component at all. The translation therefore happens in the CHUNK, exactly as
// `mrt_host_wall_clock` translates nanoseconds into a FILETIME — one lane's vocabulary becoming the op's
// contract, rather than the op's contract becoming per-lane.
export let PosixErrnoNoSuchFileOrDirectory = 2
export let PosixErrnoOperationNotPermitted = 1
export let PosixErrnoPermissionDenied = 13
export let PosixErrnoNotADirectory = 20
export let PosixErrnoResultTooLarge = 34

// ⭐⭐ **A CLEARED errno MEANS "THE WALK RAN OUT", AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES `ERROR_NO_MORE_FILES` REACHABLE
// WITHOUT A PRIVATE SENTINEL.** `FindNextFileA` reports end-of-iteration by FAILING with
// `ERROR_NO_MORE_FILES`; POSIX reports it by an exhausted enumeration that sets no errno at all. So each
// lane's walk clears errno before every step and the classifier reads a zero as exactly that
// end-of-iteration — which is sound because `osLastError` is only ever consumed on a path where a call has
// just FAILED, and the one failure in this whole surface that sets no errno is this one.
export let PosixErrnoCleared = 0

// The catch-all an errno with no Win32 twin becomes — `ERROR_GEN_FAILURE`. It deliberately is not one of the
// three the Std classifier tests, so every unmapped failure takes that classification's per-operation
// fallback (`openFailed`, `readFailed`, …) instead of impersonating one of the three named variants.
export let ErrorGenericFailure = 31

// ⭐ **HOW MANY NANOSECONDS A SECOND HAS.** Every POSIX lane reads clocks whose unit is the nanosecond, and
// the Linux thread-CPU chunk needs this outright: `clock_gettime` answers a `timespec` while
// `__thread_cpu_ticks` promises one number, so that chunk scales the seconds half by this. Darwin's twin
// needs no such step — `clock_gettime_nsec_np` already answers nanoseconds.
//
// ⚠ `Arm64DarwinRuntime`'s `DarwinMonotonicTicksPerSecond` is the same digits and a DIFFERENT fact — the
// frequency that chunk REPORTS through an out-param — which is why the two are not one name.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS ALSO THE FREQUENCY `mrt_host_monotonic_clock` REPORTS ON EVERY POSIX LANE, AS A CONSTANT**,
// because the reading beside it is already nanoseconds — which makes `__gt_now_ns`'s ticks→nanoseconds
// scaling the IDENTITY there, the strongest form that split can take and one with no rounding of its own.
export let PosixNanosPerSecond = 1000000000

// ⭐⭐ **NANOSECONDS PER FILETIME TICK — the one FILETIME fact the Std tier does not already export, and it
// is DERIVED rather than written down.** Every POSIX wall clock must hand `osReadWallClock`'s consumer a
// Win32 FILETIME, so each divides a nanosecond reading by this; the OTHER two facts of that encoding
// (`FileTimeTicksPerSecond`, `FileTimeTicksFrom1601To1970`) are `ClockRuntime`'s exports and are read from
// there rather than restated. An emitter that reads the consumer's own constants cannot be a second
// calendar, and a quotient that is COMPUTED cannot disagree with its operands.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A FUNCTION AND NOT A `let` FOR A LANGUAGE REASON, NOT A STYLE ONE**: a global initializer is a
// constant expression, and `/` there folds as a FLOAT (E3009 on the cast, measured). Inside a body it is the
// integer divide the two constants deserve — `RegBits.runtimeFrameSlots` is the same shape.
export function posixNanosPerFileTimeTick() returns MachineWord
	return (PosixNanosPerSecond / FileTimeTicksPerSecond) as MachineWord
end 'posixNanosPerFileTimeTick'

// ⭐ **HOW MANY BYTES OF A PATH-SHAPED STRING FIT BESIDE ITS TERMINATOR.** Three things are bounded by it and
// all three are `char[MAX_PATH]` in the Win32 surface being emulated: `WIN32_FIND_DATAA`'s `cFileName`, the
// copy of the pattern a search owns for its lifetime, and the directory prefix a glob is split into. One
// name, because they are one number for one reason.
export let PosixPathStringCapacity = MaxPathBytes - 1

// The permission bits a fresh named segment is created with: owner read/write, nobody else. A trace ring
// carries a program's allocation history, and the two processes that share it run as the same user. POSIX
// numbers the mode bits identically on both lanes, so the create's ONE differing argument is its flag word.
export let PosixSharedSegmentMode = 0o600

// The mode word an open that CREATES NOTHING must carry. ⛔ It is not merely "unused": Linux REFUSES an
// `openat` whose mode is non-zero without `O_CREAT` (`build_open_flags`, -EINVAL), so this is a required
// argument value rather than a placeholder — which is why it is named rather than left as a bare 0 beside
// the `O_RDONLY` that is also 0 and is a different fact.
export let PosixNoFileMode = 0

// ⭐ **`INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE` as the two's-complement word a `mov` materialises.**
// `ManagedFileRuntime.InvalidHandleValue` is the same -1 expressed for the Std tier, and `MmapAnonFd` is the
// same spelling one file over for the same reason: `MachineWord` is unsigned.
export let PosixInvalidHandle = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

// ⭐ **THE POSIX PRIORITY TRIPLE, AND THE ONE PLACE IT IS WRITTEN.** `setpriority(which, who, value)` /
// `getpriority(which, who)`: `PRIO_PROCESS` selects the PROCESS scope (read from this host's
// `sys/resource.h`, not from memory) and `who = 0` names the calling one — MEASURED to be 0 on an aarch64
// Linux guest (`bits/resource.h`) and on an x86-64 one, which is why one pair serves all three POSIX lanes.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE TWO OSes DISAGREE ABOUT WHAT `PRIO_PROCESS` SCOPES TO, AND THE NAME HIDES IT.** Darwin scopes a
// nice value to the PROCESS, which discharges `StdOp.osThreadCreate`'s inheritance contract there with
// nothing emitted at a thread's birth. LINUX scopes it to the calling THREAD — `who` is a TID, not a PID
// (MEASURED: one thread at nice 15 while its sibling sat at 10, same process, simultaneously). That is why
// each `lowerOsEnterBackgroundPriority` asks its route oracle rather than assuming POSIX is one thing, and
// why `processPriority` is its OWN row in `targetProvidesFacility`.
//
// ⚠ On both Linux lanes the disagreement HAS a consequence, and it is bounded rather than absent: `clone`
// inherits its creator's nice value at birth, so every M started after the call runs at the priority asked
// for, and only an M that already existed keeps the default. The two `processPriority` rows in
// `targetProvidesFacility` carry it beside the `true` that rests on it.
export let PosixPrioProcess = 0
export let PosixPriorityWhoIsSelf = 0

// The nice value background work runs at. ⚠ NOT 19, the maximum, for the reason Windows does not use
// `IDLE_PRIORITY_CLASS`: a starve long enough to trip a caller's timeout is reported as a harness bug
// rather than as a busy machine. The bootstrap's arm64 emitter picks the same 10
// (`ARM64CodeEmitter.Backend.cs`), so the two compilers de-prioritise a suite to the same depth.
export let PosixBackgroundNiceValue = 10

// ==========================================================================
// struct stat — the ONE thing that is not shared
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **THE FIVE FIELDS THIS SURFACE READS, AND WHERE EACH LANE PUTS THEM.** Every offset is MEASURED on the
// lane that declares it, and the lanes agree on almost nothing — `st_size` is at 96 on Darwin and 48 on
// Linux, and the two Linux arches disagree about the size of the structure and about `st_mode` while
// agreeing about the other four. So the chunks that read a `struct stat` take this as a parameter instead of
// being written once per lane around one set of literals each.
//
// ⚠ **A `timespec` FIELD'S OFFSET IS ITS `tv_sec`**, because `tv_sec` comes first — so the three time
// offsets address SECONDS directly and the nanosecond half is simply never read: a FILETIME's consumer
// (`ManagedFileRuntime.emitFileTimeToUnixSeconds`) divides back down to whole seconds anyway.
//
// ⚠ **`creationTimeOffset` IS THE ONE FIELD THE TWO OSes DISAGREE ABOUT IN MEANING, NOT MERELY IN PLACE.**
// Darwin has a real birth time (`st_birthtime`); Linux's `struct stat` has none at all — only `statx` does —
// so its lanes name `st_ctime`, the inode's last status change, which is the closest thing a `stat` can
// answer and is what `File.info().createdTime` reports there.
export type PosixStatLayout
	// `sizeof(struct stat)`, from which the frame slots it occupies are derived.
	export let bytes as ParsedInt
	// `st_mode`, read as a 32-bit word and masked by `PosixModeMask`. Must be 4-aligned.
	export let modeOffset as MachineWord
	// The remaining four are 64-bit reads and must be 8-aligned.
	export let sizeOffset as MachineWord
	export let creationTimeOffset as MachineWord
	export let accessTimeOffset as MachineWord
	export let modifyTimeOffset as MachineWord

	export static function create(bytes ParsedInt, modeOffset MachineWord, sizeOffset MachineWord, creationTimeOffset MachineWord, accessTimeOffset MachineWord, modifyTimeOffset MachineWord) returns PosixStatLayout
		return Self{bytes: bytes, modeOffset: modeOffset, sizeOffset: sizeOffset, creationTimeOffset: creationTimeOffset, accessTimeOffset: accessTimeOffset, modifyTimeOffset: modifyTimeOffset}
	end 'create'
end 'PosixStatLayout'

// ==========================================================================
// The POSIX HOST-OBJECT PROTOCOL — the numbers every POSIX lane's bodies agree on
// ==========================================================================
//
// ⭐⭐ **NEITHER ARCH NOR OS HAS A SAY IN ANY OF THESE, WHICH IS WHY THEY LIVE HERE AND NOT WITH AN
// ASSEMBLER.** A wait object's field offsets, a handle's kind tag, the bits `poll` answers with and the way
// POSIX packs an exit status into a wait word are read by the arm64 chunks and by the x86-64 ones alike;
// each backend writes its own INSTRUCTIONS around them and none of them gets to choose a different number.
// Two copies of `PosixWaitOffChildPid` is a handle whose pid one lane writes and the other reads four bytes
// away from — a wrong answer with no compile error between the two files.

// ⭐ **THE WAIT OBJECT'S KIND, AS THE ISEL SPELLS IT.** `osEventCreate` lowers to `mrt_host_wait_new(kind)`
// on BOTH lanes and must name the same number on each, so the value is a shared protocol rather than either
// lane's private constant. The other kinds are not here because no isel lowering names one: a thread object
// is minted inside `mrt_host_thread_create` and a child process inside `mrt_host_spawn`, each of which knows
// its own kind (`Arm64DarwinRuntime`'s layout header enumerates all three).
export let PosixWaitKindEvent = 1

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`StdOp.osCloseHandle` CLOSES FOUR DIFFERENT THINGS AND POSIX HAS NO TYPE IN COMMON FOR THEM.** On
// Windows a wake event, a worker thread, a pipe end, an opened file and a child process are all one opaque
// `HANDLE`, and one `CloseHandle` serves every one of them. On either POSIX lane the first two and the last
// are heap objects the backend mints, the middle two are DESCRIPTORS, and "close" means `free`/`munmap` for
// one and `close` for the other. A value that is only a number cannot say which — and the numbers OVERLAP,
// because a small descriptor and an allocated pointer are both just integers.
//
// ⇒ **A handle on every POSIX lane carries its kind in bits 48 and up**, which a user virtual address never
// occupies on any of the three: arm64-macOS is 47-bit, arm64-Linux is 48-bit (so bit 47 is the highest one
// used), and on Linux/x86-64 a user VA is `< 2^47` under 4-level paging while under LA57 the kernel hands out
// an address at or above `2^47` only to an explicit high `mmap` hint, which nothing in this compiler passes.
// `mrt_host_wait_close` reads those bits and dispatches; everything that consumes a DESCRIPTOR masks them
// off, which costs two instructions and is a no-op for the untagged descriptors `osFileOpenRead` and
// `osStdHandle` already answer.
//
// ⚠ **KIND 0 IS THE DESCRIPTOR KIND ON PURPOSE**, so a raw fd from any other family is already a valid
// handle and needs no tagging at its source — and so that the Std tier's `0 means no handle` sentinel
// (which `emitSubpCloseSlotHandles` relies on, and which is `CloseHandle(NULL)`'s Win32 no-op) keeps
// meaning exactly that.
//
// ⛔ **⇒ EVERY LANE's `mrt_host_wait_close` MUST GUARD A ZERO DESCRIPTOR, AND THE GUARD IS LOAD-BEARING
// RATHER THAN DEFENSIVE.** `SubprocessRuntime.emitSubpCloseSlotHandles` closes every slot field
// unconditionally; on Windows `CloseHandle(0)` is a no-op by the API's own contract, while here fd 0 is
// STANDARD INPUT and closing it would take the program's stdin away.
export let PosixHandleKindShift = 48 as ShiftCount
let PosixHandleKindDescriptor = 0
export let PosixHandleKindObject = 1

// How WIDE the kind field is — the bits above the 48-bit payload, which on every POSIX lane is every bit a
// user virtual address cannot reach (see above for each lane's reason).
export let PosixHandleKindBits = (MachineWordBits - PosixHandleKindShift) as ShiftCount

// `1 << PosixHandleKindShift` as a value rather than a shift, so a kind can be turned into a tag by a
// multiply the `movz #imm, lsl #48` ladder encodes in one instruction.
export let PosixHandleKindUnit = 0x0001000000000000

// The byte count an unset variable answers — `GetEnvironmentVariableA`'s own 0, and the value both lanes seed
// `writtenSlot` with before their lookup so the not-found arm needs no branch of its own.
export let PosixEnvUnset = 0

export let PosixMillisPerSecond = 1000

// The other direction of the same fact, and the unit each lane's millisecond-to-`timespec` split
// (`arm64PosixMillisToTimespec`, `x64LinuxEmitMillisToTimespec`) turns a remainder into. ⚠ `mrt_host_tick_ms` does NOT use it: that chunk derives its divisor from the FREQUENCY
// `mrt_host_monotonic_clock` reports, so a lane that ever answered raw counter ticks would still be right.
export let PosixNanosPerMilli = 1000000

// What a POSIX call that reports only success or failure answers on success.
export let PosixCallSucceeded = 0

// ⭐ **A CHILD PROCESS IS A WAIT OBJECT KIND, AND BOTH LANES NOW MINT ONE**, so it joins `PosixWaitKindEvent`
// here rather than staying either lane's private constant: `mrt_host_spawn` writes it and `mrt_host_wait_one`
// and `mrt_host_wait_close` both dispatch on it, in bodies this file owns.
export let PosixWaitKindProcess = 3

// ⭐⭐ **AND SO IS A WORKER M's OS THREAD.** Every POSIX lane's `mrt_host_thread_create` mints one and every
// lane's `mrt_host_wait_one` and `mrt_host_wait_close` dispatch on it, so it is a shared protocol number like
// the two above rather than any one lane's private constant. ⚠ The three kinds are 1, 2 and 3 and none is 0,
// so an object whose kind field was never written cannot be mistaken for any of them.
export let PosixWaitKindThread = 2

// ==========================================================================
// The WAIT OBJECT's shared prefix
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **SIX FIELDS AT SIX FIXED OFFSETS, IDENTICAL ON BOTH LANES — which is what lets the reap, the await,
// the exit-code read and the multi-wait be ONE body each.** What the two lanes disagree about is what sits
// ABOVE these: Darwin follows them with a `pthread_mutex_t` and a `pthread_cond_t` (an event there is a
// condition variable); Linux follows them with nothing at all (a futex needs no mutex, and the object is one
// `mmap`'d page).
//
// ⭐⭐ **0x10 IS THE OS THREAD's OWN IDENTITY, AND EVERY LANE PUTS SOMETHING DIFFERENT THERE.** A `pthread_t`
// that `pthread_join` takes on Darwin, the `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` word the kernel zeroes and wakes on Linux —
// but the ROLE is identical (it is what `mrt_host_wait_one`'s THREAD arm joins through), so the offset is one
// fact written once rather than lanes agreeing by hand. ⚠ Only a THREAD object gives it a MEANING; on the
// other two kinds it is a zero, written explicitly on the lane whose allocator does not zero (`malloc`) and
// inherited from the page on the lane whose does (`mmap`).
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **`PosixWaitOffChildStatus` EXISTS BECAUSE A POSIX WAIT CONSUMES THE STATUS AND WIN32's TWO CALLS DO
// NOT.** `WaitForSingleObject(hProcess)` and `GetExitCodeProcess(hProcess)` are both repeatable and
// independent; POSIX fuses them into one call that can be answered exactly ONCE — MEASURED on both lanes, a
// second wait on the same pid answers -1/`ECHILD`. Every Std graph in the subprocess family polls the handle
// and reads the code separately, and several do both more than once. ⇒ `mrt_host_proc_reap` is the ONE place
// a status is taken, it CACHES it in this field, and every later ask is served from there. That is the whole
// reason a child-process handle on a POSIX lane is an object rather than the bare pid it could otherwise be.
export let PosixWaitOffKind = 0x00 as MachineWord
export let PosixWaitOffSignal = 0x08 as MachineWord
export let PosixWaitOffThread = 0x10 as MachineWord
export let PosixWaitOffChildPid = 0x18 as MachineWord
export let PosixWaitOffChildStatus = 0x20 as MachineWord
export let PosixWaitOffChildKilled = 0x28 as MachineWord

// The two states of the signal word above. They are HERE rather than once per lane because the field is
// shared and `mrt_host_proc_reap` — a shared body — publishes into it; each lane's own event protocol reads
// the same two values through its own primitive (a condvar predicate on Darwin, a `FUTEX_WAIT` comparand on
// Linux).
//
// ⭐⭐ **TWO STATES AND NOT A COUNT, BECAUSE `SetEvent` IS AN AUTO-RESET EVENT'S CONTRACT.** `mrt_host_event_set`
// publishes with a COMPARE-AND-SWAP whose verdict is discarded, which is correct rather than a missed signal:
// it can fail only when the word is already signalled, and `SetEvent` on an already-signalled auto-reset
// event is a documented no-op. A counting increment would release TWO waiters for two signals — a worker M
// woken for work another M has already taken.
//
// ⚠ **THE WAKE HAPPENS WHETHER OR NOT THE PUBLISH LANDED.** A waiter that observed the signalled state and is
// about to sleep is refused by its own primitive's compare; one already asleep is woken by the wake.
export let PosixWaitNotSignalled = 0
export let PosixWaitSignalled = 1

// ==========================================================================
// The POSIX facts the child-process band reads — MEASURED on BOTH lanes
// ==========================================================================

// `struct pollfd` is `{int fd; short events; short revents;}` — EIGHT bytes, MEASURED on Darwin and on an
// aarch64 Linux guest, which is why the whole descriptor is written and read as ONE 64-bit word rather than
// through three stores of three widths. Little-endian: `fd` is the low half, `events` the third halfword,
// `revents` the fourth.
export let PosixPollIn = 1
export let PosixPollEventsUnit = 0x0000000100000000
export let PosixPollReventsShift = 48 as ShiftCount
export let PosixPollOneDescriptor = 1

// `poll`'s revents bits, MEASURED on both. Only the two that mean *"this descriptor is unusable"* are tested
// — `POLLHUP` (0x10) is deliberately NOT among them, because a pipe whose writer has closed reports it
// ALONGSIDE `POLLIN` while bytes are still buffered (MEASURED: `revents = 0x11` on both lanes), and treating
// it as broken would discard them.
let PosixPollErr = 8
let PosixPollNval = 32
export let PosixPollBrokenBits = PosixPollErr or PosixPollNval

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT `mrt_host_pipe_peek` REPORTS AS "AVAILABLE", AND WHY IT IS NOT THE TRUE COUNT.**
// `PeekNamedPipe` answers a byte count; the honest POSIX twin would be `ioctl(fd, FIONREAD, &n)`, and NO
// POSIX lane issues it — a syscall and an out-param slot per drain pass, for a number the one consumer
// discards before it uses it.
//
// It costs nothing, because of what that ONE consumer does with the answer. `emitSubpDrainStream` uses it only
// to (a) decide whether to read at all and (b) size one pass, which it immediately clamps to
// `SubpReadChunkBytes`; the bytes actually moved come from the READ. So reporting "a pipe-buffer's worth"
// makes the pass exactly one clamped chunk and the read reports the truth — while a peek that answered `1`
// would drip one byte per pass and turn an N-byte capture into N round trips.
//
// ⚠ **AND IT IS WHY EOF ARRIVES THROUGH THE READ RATHER THAN THROUGH THE PEEK.** `emitSubpDrainStream` has
// two edges into `markDone`: a FAILED peek (Win32's broken-pipe reading) and a read of 0 bytes. A pipe whose
// writer has closed polls READABLE on both lanes — MEASURED `0x11` with bytes buffered and `0x10` once
// drained — so this answers "readable", the read answers 0, and the second edge fires.
//
// ⚠ The number is also the pipe's real capacity on both (MEASURED: 65536 on aarch64 Linux, and the threshold
// at which a wait-before-drain deadlocks on either).
export let PosixPipeReadyBytes = 65536

// The two ways to ask `mrt_host_proc_reap` for a status, and the "do not block" option it hands the wait.
export let PosixReapWithoutBlocking = 0
export let PosixReapBlocking = 1
export let PosixWaitNoHang = 1

// Decoding a wait status, which POSIX defines by macro rather than by field: the low seven bits are the
// terminating SIGNAL and are zero for a normal exit, in which case bits 8..15 are the exit code. MEASURED
// identical on both lanes (`0x700` for exit 7, `0x9` for SIGKILL).
export let PosixWaitStatusSignalMask = 0x7F
export let PosixWaitStatusExitShift = 8 as ShiftCount
export let PosixWaitStatusExitMask = 0xFF

// ⚠ **A SIGNAL-KILLED CHILD REPORTS `128 + signal`, WHICH IS THE SHELL'S CONVENTION AND NOT WIN32's.** There
// is no exit code for a process the kernel killed, and every POSIX shell answers this. The one place the two
// lanes WOULD have disagreed with Windows is the deadline kill, and they do not:
// `TerminateProcess(h, code)` names the code the handle must then report, and `mrt_host_proc_terminate`
// records it (`childKilled`) so the reap below hands back that code rather than `128 + SIGKILL`.
export let PosixSignalExitCodeBase = 128
export let PosixSigKill = 9

// The two answers the reap gives, named because a bare 0/1 at four call sites says nothing about which
// direction it reads.
export let PosixChildSignalled = 1
export let PosixChildStillRunning = 0

// A C `int`'s worth of bits — `pid_t`'s width, which is what a wait's answer must be compared at.
export let PosixChildPidMask = 0xFFFFFFFF

// How long the two bounded child waits sleep between scans — the netpoll's multi-wait and a
// `waitWithTimeout` deadline. Both run where the M is meant to be idle, so a millisecond is a poll rate
// rather than a spin, and one number keeps the two from drifting into different responsiveness.
export let PosixChildPollMs = 1

// `GtProcHandlesBaseLabel`'s array is one 64-bit handle per entry, so an index scales by 8 — as a SHIFT,
// which is what an indexed load off a base wants.
export let PosixHandleArrayStrideShift = 3 as ShiftCount

// The child's three standard descriptors, named because a bare 0/1/2 in an argument position says nothing.
export let PosixChildStdinFd = 0
export let PosixChildStdoutFd = 1
export let PosixChildStderrFd = 2

// `PROCESS_INFORMATION.hThread` on either POSIX lane. Neither hands back a thread handle for a spawned
// process, and the Std graph closes this field unconditionally — which `mrt_host_wait_close` reads as
// "no handle".
export let PosixNoThreadHandle = 0

// What `CreatePipe`'s two out-params are set to when the pipe could not be created. The Std tier's `0 means
// no handle` sentinel is what makes a failed pipe safe to close unconditionally, exactly as
// `PosixNoThreadHandle` does for a spawn.
export let PosixNoPipeDescriptor = 0

// What `PeekNamedPipe` reports available when the descriptor is readable-but-empty. Not a failure: the peek
// still answers TRUE, and `PosixPipeReadyBytes` carries what the count means.
export let PosixNoBytesReady = 0

// `PROCESS_INFORMATION.hProcess` and `.dwProcessId` when the spawn failed. A zero `hProcess` IS the Std
// tier's own spawn-failure test, so this is the report and not merely the absence of one.
export let PosixNoProcessHandle = 0
export let PosixNoProcessId = 0

// `int fds[2]` — the WRITE end is the second `int`, four bytes past the first.
export let PosixPipeWriteEndOffset = 4

// `poll`'s "answer now" timeout, as the `int` milliseconds it takes.
export let PosixPollNoWaitMillis = 0

// `ppoll`'s `sigmask` argument. NULL leaves the calling thread's signal mask alone, which is what `poll` does
// and all this surface wants — this backend installs no signal handlers at all.
export let PosixPollNoSignalMask = 0

// `wait4`'s `struct rusage *` out-param, which NULL discards — and the register `waitpid` never reads.
export let PosixReapDiscardsRusage = 0
